00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.230
Starting with a
good introduction

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.990
around persistent disk--

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.720
with GCP, you get many options
around how you store data.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.600
You get databases and
many kinds of databases

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.020
with in-memory relation
databases, NoSQL data

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:25.940
warehouses.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.790
And on the storage side, we
have block, object, and file.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.500
In this session, we
are mainly focusing

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.970
on block storage for GCP,
which is a persistent disk.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.090
Persistent disk is a highly
durable, high performance block

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.890
storage for GCP.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.040
And a couple of key facts
around block storage

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.620
is it's simple,
fast, and flexible.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.440
The reason I say it's
simple, fast, and flexible

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.650
are around the meat of,
do we need striping?

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.730
Does it do a lot of the
stuff on the back end

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:59.850
versus forcing the customers
to do OS level optimizations?

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.220
It provides a
virtual SCSI device,

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.550
and it can scale from one gig
to a 64 terabyte size itself.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.880
Getting into the block
storage ties, with GCP,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.400
we have three different
types of block storage.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.320
One is a local SSD.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.170
A local SSD can go
up to 3 terabytes.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.980
It's mainly recommended as a
high performance scratch space

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.500
because it's an
epithermal storage.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.950
So it's local to the
VM, and it's essentially

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:28.760
a scratch space.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.520
And it's priced around
$0.08 per gig per month.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.610
Now when we get into
network-based PD,

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.030
we have two different options.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.930
One is standard persistent
disk and SSD persistent disk.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.840
The use cases around
them for standard

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.750
are more around large
data processing workloads

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.390
around [? dynamics ?]
processing and video editing.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:50.150
And it's essentially
targeted towards a price

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.090
sensitive audience with
$0.04 per gig per month.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.470
When we get into
SSD, that is focused

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.740
a lot on high
performance databases

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.860
like MySQL, SQL servers,
Oracle, or any other database

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.370
of choice.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.780
It is persistent,
and it usually runs

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:12.320
around $0.17 per gig per month
for the SSD persistent disk.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.180
There are some also
data management features

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.100
around it you can have.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.635
It comes standard
with encryption.

00:03:17.635 --> 00:03:18.635
It comes with snapshots.

00:03:18.635 --> 00:03:20.180
So I'm going to
talk later on what

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.580
different types of
snapshots are there.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.550
And so the scalability
functions are you can actually

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.120
have 128 PDs per VM.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.540
And each PD can be
around 64 terabyte size.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.850
Couple of common
patterns how we think PD

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.950
is used generally
by customers-- you

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.690
can use PD as a boot device.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.320
You can use PD as a
boot device and use it

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.490
as a read write
volumes for your VMs.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.830
PD also allows to
have a [? detached ?]

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.020
into multiple VMs as
a read only device.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.210
Stanley will be covering
a couple of more use cases

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.870
around specifically how you can
use PD for data distribution

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.670
around multiple VMs.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.180
And we also have a new feature,
which we're releasing soon,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.060
is around synchronous
replicated PDs across zones.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.240
So this provides
active-active applications

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.600
across two zones in the same
region in case of a PD failure.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.019
And I'll be covering more
details in the following slides

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:17.130
on that front too.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.170
But these are the
common patterns

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:22.380
we see around how PD is attached
to VM for different workload

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.540
optimizations.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.510
Getting more detail into-- so
the data management features.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.090
We covered multi-reader support.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.550
So you can use PD in a
read only mode to share

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.550
data across multiple VMs.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.110
PD snapshots are geo-replicated.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.320
You can take regional and
multi-regional snapshots,

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.100
and I'll talk more
about that too.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.710
You can scale without
interruptions.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.320
So talking about
simplicity and flexibility,

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:51.340
you can actually scale a PD
without having to detach it.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.560
You can scale it dynamically
while it's attached.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.250
And of course, all PD comes
with automatic encryption

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.880
across the board.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.610
Getting into more data
protection features,

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.670
PD has snapshots, and
the standard snapshots

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.010
are copied over into GCS.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.440
So when you take a first
snapshot, it is full.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.580
And every other snapshot
can be incremental.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.400
Now once the PD snapshot
is taken, you can choose.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.560
Do you want to store
them in a regional bucket

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.950
or a multi-regional bucket?

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.390
One of the features that was
asked for a long time and it

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:34.390
just went into production is
around PD snapshot scheduling.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.700
With snapshot scheduling,
you can actually

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.800
control the lifecycle
of a snapshot.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.640
How many snapshots
do you want to take?

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.240
How often do you want
to take and how long

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.380
do you retain them?

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.270
So you can define
a retention policy.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.420
You can define a
snapshot creation policy,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.700
and it can all work
offline without having

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.540
you to schedule with scripts or
other tools outside of the core

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:01.360
PD pieces.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.080
Getting into a bit more
around snapshot location,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.080
snapshot location is a
feature which is in beta.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.140
And this is more for
customers who are

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.392
focused around data locality.

00:06:13.392 --> 00:06:15.100
So if you're taking
a snapshot and you're

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.410
concerned about that my
snapshot shouldn't leave

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:20.950
certain localities or
you want to maintain

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.400
for either compliance or
taxation or different purposes

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.980
that corporations
have, you can actually

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:31.070
control precisely where
your snapshot is stored.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.300
You can say, my snapshot
is taken from US Central

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.670
and can go to US East 1.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.550
Or you can say, my
snapshot stays in US,

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.960
and I want to store them in
a multi-regional US bucket.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.640
So this is truly
targeted towards where

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.590
you want to control
data locality

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.360
but still want to control--

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:51.620
have DR with snapshot.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.170
Another feature which is going
GA end of the month-- it should

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:03.350
be in our blog post too-- is
around synchronous replication.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:09.770
So PD has regional PD, which
takes two copies of a PD

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:12.350
in two different zones,
and all the rights

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.210
are synchronized
in the back end.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.980
So before a write is sent
across back to the host

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.160
as acknowledged,
the write is exactly

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.230
synchronized across both sides.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:23.840
This particular feature
is more targeted

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.240
towards applications
which are highly

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.390
sensitive to respond to
recovery point and recovery time

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:30.501
objectives.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:38.220
Now getting into
performance basics,

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.110
we can look at performance in
multiple different dimensions.

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:44.700
One dimension that I like
to deep dive is IO density.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.110
So when you look at IOPS, PD
standard provides 0.75 reads

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.870
and 1.5 write IOPS per gig.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.210
And this metric is
particularly important

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.660
if you look at what's
the maximum disk

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:02.010
size do I need to achieve
the full IOs that I

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.470
need for my application.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.060
Same thing for PD-SSD--

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.390
you can get up to
30 IOPS per gig.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:09.960
And in the following
charts, I'll

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.790
show you kind of what's the ramp
up for PD size versus the IOPS

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:17.220
and megabytes per second ramp.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.470
One of the latest changes
around PD standard

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:24.030
is PD standard had a bandwidth
of 80 megabits per second

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:27.180
for read and 120 for writes.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.470
It just moved up to 240
reads and 240 writes.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.230
So that should help with a
lot of throughput sensitive

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.760
applications.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.110
For PD-SSD, we're looking
at 1.2 gigs per second reads

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.840
and 400 megabytes
per second write.

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:49.220
Now I talked about IO density
in terms of megabytes in IOPS.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:51.320
And if you look at this
chart, you literally

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.380
can reach peak
throughput in terms

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:58.370
of megabits per second
for a standard PD

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.310
but by adapting a
2 terabyte drive.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.920
Same thing for if
you are using PD-SSD

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.580
for IO sensitive applications,
you can achieve the peak with 2

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.410
terabytes also.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:11.420
So that essentially
means you can actually

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:16.280
optimize for throughput
or IOPS depending

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:19.220
on what your application
needs without having

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.600
tons of spare capacity to
get to that max number.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.540
Another criteria
when looking at what

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:29.300
are the different thresholds
to achieve the peak performance

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:33.800
is what's the size of VM that
I need to get to that peak.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.130
Now if you look at
PD standard, you only

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:41.190
need four vCPUs to achieve
the max performance

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.150
on a PD standard.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:47.350
And for PD-SSD, you can get
up to there with 32 vCPUs,

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:51.570
which is pretty aggressive in
terms of spare CPU cycles that

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:53.760
you would have to spend
getting to that levels,

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:55.800
and the slopes are--

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.260
you can get to the peak
with just 32 in PD-SSD.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:05.060
I'm going to transfer
control to Stanley for more

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:06.530
details around performance.

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:09.433
STANLEY FENG: Thank you,
Manu, for the introduction.

00:10:09.433 --> 00:10:10.100
Hello, everyone.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:11.900
A brief introduction
about myself--

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.040
I work on performance
of the persistent disk

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.730
and focus on
performance of the disk

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.760
itself and also try to
optimize performance

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.450
for different kind of workloads.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:24.470
So today, the first
section will be

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:27.110
focused around performance
recommendations, what

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.570
you can do to best utilize PD.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:33.770
So let's first start with
a brief, very high level,

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:37.610
30,000 feet view of the
persistent disk architecture.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.920
As you can see here,
you have GC VMs,

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.760
and you have persistent disk.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.230
And this shows up as a
disk to the GC VMs, which

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.850
are the rectangle boxes.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:50.960
And two key high level takeaways
you can have on this graph

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:55.080
is, one, this PD is
a virtualized disk.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:56.900
It's completely virtualized.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.200
Every copy of data-- say, for
example, look at the blue copy.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.150
If you write a
logical copy to PD,

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:05.360
it's sent back down
to a massive scale

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.710
back end physical
storage system.

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:11.800
And it's replicated three
way or three way equivalent.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:14.720
And then because it's
completely virtualized,

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:16.760
it does not have a
one to one mapping

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.110
from a logical processor disk to
a particular physical resource.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:24.980
And it allows us to
innovate and define

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.680
some sort of unique
feature that is

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:29.360
unlikely to happen
in a completely

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.120
physical attached disk world.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:35.990
And the other takeaway
you can have on this

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.120
is because we're built on top of
a Google internal massive scale

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:43.490
distributor file system,
it allows us to scale PD.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:47.480
And we do a lot of
optimization for random writes.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.370
And also in terms
of reads, we try

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:53.550
to read from the fastest
copy of those three.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:56.210
So move on to some of
the additional details

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.270
about performance, and these
are the details that we actually

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.370
mentioned in our documentation.

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:03.920
But sometimes, we found
out lots of customers

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:07.140
often didn't pay much
attention to those.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.050
So first, for the IOPS
and throughput limits,

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.490
when we published those limits,
in order to measure IOPS,

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:15.980
you use 8 kB IOs or less.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:17.720
And then to measure
throughput, make

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:21.290
sure you use 256
kB IOs or larger

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:23.540
and use sequential IOs to match.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.580
And the third point
is very interesting is

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:29.580
that if you attach multiple
disks of the same type

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:31.820
but different sizes
to the same VM,

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:34.670
the individual disk
can actually achieve

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:38.060
the same level of performance
as the sum of those disks.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:42.200
For example, if you attach
one 10 gigabytes disk and then

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:46.040
1 terabytes disk to the same
VM, the 10 gigabyte disk

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.020
can actually perform
at the same level

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:52.440
as 1 terabyte plus 10 gigabyte
if you're using the 10 gigabyte

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:53.180
disk only.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:56.860
This gives you great
flexibility in terms of IOs

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:58.720
and in terms of
how you use those.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:01.490
As long as you buy
enough size, you

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:04.480
will get those IOs
regardless on which disk

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.250
you use those IOs
on for that VM.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:08.900
Then this last
point, which I will

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:10.940
talk in great detail
in the next slide,

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.250
is about the cost of duplex.

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:17.620
Duplex means you can achieve the
read write limit concurrently

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.070
as a max.

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:22.690
So for PD-SSDs, the throughput
limit is actually duplex.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.430
That means you can achieve the
read write limit as documented

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:26.960
at the same time at max.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:30.350
But the rest of the other limits
like PD standard throughput

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:31.280
is not duplex.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:33.210
IOPS limit is also duplex.

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:34.800
Let's take a look at the detail.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.850
So again, this big
table and a lot of data

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:40.940
is actually copy pasted
from our documentation.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:43.700
And throughout the
years, customers

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:47.700
have had questions to us and
were asking what does this mean

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:48.590
and what is duplex.

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:51.200
So I figure it's worth
calling out today

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:54.230
and to just give a quick
example and the introduction

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:55.490
of this concept.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:59.510
And so full duplex means maximum
limits for read and write

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.930
can be achieved simultaneously.

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:04.320
If you look at the lower-right
corner of this graph,

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:05.640
as you can see--

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:06.210
this table.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.085
As you can see, it's
very easy to understand.

00:14:08.085 --> 00:14:11.000
For SSD read write
throughput limit,

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:14.600
you can achieve 240
megabytes at the same time.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:17.750
If you keep hitting the disk
with maximum read and write,

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:19.550
both sides can reach 240.

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:22.280
Now what does not duplex mean?

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.080
Not duplex means
you can take a look

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.290
at the example of the
standard persistent disk read

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:28.440
write limits.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.950
So if you do read
only, you can do 240.

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:32.930
If you do write
only, you can do 240.

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:35.940
But what happens if you do
read or write at the same time?

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:39.380
Now let's assume if you
have 50% reads, 50% writes,

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:41.270
then proportionately,
both limits

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:45.350
will be reduced to the
middle, which is 120 and 120.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:50.210
So that's how you can
understand read write mixed,

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:51.950
which is called non-duplex.

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.870
So of all these limits--

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:57.740
IOPS limit, throughput limits--
only the PD-SSD throughput

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:01.600
limit is full
duplex, so which is,

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:06.330
you can reach 1.2 [? G4 ?]
reads at max and 400 megabytes

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.340
for writes.

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:11.910
So now let's switch topic
to application level tuning

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:15.150
and what you can do at a high
level in your application

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.850
to make sure you can
fully utilize the disk.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.970
So first of all,
always try to ensure

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:25.080
that you issue enough concurrent
IOs to reach maximum IOPS.

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.450
So the number of
concurrent IOs is sometimes

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:28.560
also known as queue depth.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.690
If you use FIO frequent enough,
you will know the concept.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:34.410
So always try to ensure
queue depth of at least one

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.850
for every 800 to 1,000 IOPS.

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:39.690
For example, if we want
to hit 12,000 IOPS,

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.750
then you want to make sure the
application can have at least

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.970
15 IOs outstanding at any time.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:47.760
So if you are running
a MySQL database,

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:49.440
probably that means
you probably want

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:53.380
to configure your page cleaner
thread to be 15 or above.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:57.330
So there will always be
that many IOs outstanding.

00:15:57.330 --> 00:16:01.710
So for maximum throughput, make
sure you use 256 kB or larger

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.530
IOs and two sequentials.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:06.150
For your typical
Hadoop workload,

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.410
that shouldn't be a problem.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:12.460
So the next topic is about
enabling DISCARD or TRIM.

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:16.710
And for many of you who have a
physical world SSD experience,

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.710
you know TRIM command
or DISCARD command

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:22.590
is highly recommended for
physical SS device which

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:26.880
helps them to reduce the
write amplification effect.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:30.690
And TRIM is generally also
helpful for better performance

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.450
for PD, although it's for a
completely different reasons.

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:36.030
But the other aspect
I want to mention here

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:39.540
is about TRIM actually helps you
to reduce your persistent disk

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:43.950
snapshot size in the sense that
if you don't enable TRIM, what

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:46.740
happens is when you delete a
logical fire at the file system

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:49.650
level, file system just changes
metadata to indicate that file

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:53.190
has been deleted and the fact
that those physical blocks were

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:55.960
still appear as
used as a PD layer.

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:57.500
So if you take a
snapshot on the PD,

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.670
all those deleted
files are actually

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:01.620
also included in your snapshot.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:03.830
But if you enable
the TRIM command,

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:07.650
all those deleted blocks
would have been discarded,

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:10.630
and your snapshot size
will be much smaller.

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:12.480
So to enable trim,
you can do that dash

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:14.692
or discard option to mount.

00:17:14.692 --> 00:17:16.109
So here are a few
things you don't

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:18.880
have to do to optimize
performance for PD.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:21.480
One, this PD not an oven at all.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.730
You don't have to
pre-heat it in the sense

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.310
that for example, if you
download a restore snapshot

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:29.680
to a new PD, after the
snapshot restoration is done,

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:31.890
you can just start to
read from it directly,

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.930
and it gets full performance.

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:37.290
And then the other
aspect Manu just

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:39.810
mentioned a few
moments ago is that you

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:43.200
don't need to read
multiple PDs to reach

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:44.460
the maximum performance.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.800
Just provision a
large enough PD,

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:50.270
and that should give
you enough performance.

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:55.370
And the last one in this section
is about sharing read only data

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.470
across multiple VMs.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:17:58.970
So you can create a PD.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:02.210
You can have software packages
you want to distribute

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.640
to 10s of VMs in a zone.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:08.330
And that PD will be
in a read only mode.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:12.080
You can attach the PD using
this command dash dash mode RO

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:16.480
to a few VMs, like 10s of VMs.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:20.300
And then those VMs can all
read from that PD easily.

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:22.460
This is suitable
for a small scale

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:25.580
distribution of static
content within a zone.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.370
Now if you have a large scale,
huge scale distribution,

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:30.890
or if you want cross
zone distribution,

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:34.250
then consider using
Google Cloud Storage, GCS.

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.950
And [? all, ?] if you
have file level sharing,

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:38.520
especially read
write file sharing,

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:41.810
then we have a new product
called Google Cloud Filestore

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:43.170
you can use.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.450
So then, of course, in
this particular scenario,

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.470
PD-SSD is highly
recommended here

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.190
because you can issue lots of
read IOs to this shared PD,

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:56.610
and SSD is more suitable
for that purpose.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:19:00.230
OK, the last slide
applies if you

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:05.300
happen to use PD as a raw
device without formatting a file

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:06.690
system on top of it.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:08.240
It's called misaligned writes.

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:10.160
To basically make
sure that when you do

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.890
writes to the raw
device, the write

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:17.460
is always aligned at the
physical block size boundary.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:21.500
If you look at those 6 kB
writes that's not aligned,

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:25.730
the red block is the block
of the existing content

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:27.210
that we have to maintain.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:29.270
So the way we have
to be implement it

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:32.660
is through a process called
read-modify-write in the sense

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.430
that we have to
read the old block

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:36.920
and modify it with
your new payload

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:39.350
for the misaligned portion
and then write it back.

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:43.590
This makes the write
process extremely slow.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:46.820
So all right, so now let's move
on to the next section, which

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:50.510
is about introducing an
example of how you can optimize

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.780
a particular workload
called MySQL database using

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:56.810
a new feature called 16
kB physical block size

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:59.060
PD, which is currently in beta.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:02.300
So as we optimize performance
and introducing new features

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.160
for PD, we're always
keeping in mind

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:07.910
that we want to do it that can
actually benefit the real world

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:08.990
application.

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:10.770
And of the real
world application,

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:14.600
MySQL or database application
is fairly important to many

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:15.590
of our customers.

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:19.080
And MySQL is a very common
database application.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:21.410
So let's go over this
example, and let's

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:25.790
go over what this physical block
size, 16 kB physical block size

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.260
PD, means.

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:30.530
So for persistent--
what is a physical block

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:34.850
size, what it means in the
context of persistent disk?

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:37.640
So for persistent
disk, physical block

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:40.610
is the unit at which
writes have atomicity.

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:43.775
Atomicity Means you have
all or nothing semantics.

00:20:43.775 --> 00:20:47.360
A 16 kB write, if
it's aligned, if it's

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:50.810
returned to a 16 kB
physical block size PD,

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:53.110
you will be ensured to
either write nothing--

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:57.220
in a failure case, a VM crash,
or your application crash,

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:02.090
you'll write either nothing or
write all in a successful case.

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:04.990
Now the default physical block
size of a persistent disk

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:09.770
is 4 kB, and 16 kB physical
block size has just recently

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:14.760
been added like probably a
month ago as a beta feature.

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:16.760
So then the next
question obviously

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:20.600
is, what do I use
a 16 kB PD for?

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:23.730
Then it is really designed
for your MySQL database

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:25.430
workload that is write heavy.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:29.000
Now if you happen to run other
databases and, for example,

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:31.660
on SQL Server or Oracle
and those databases,

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:35.900
if you can change those data
page size to be 16 kB and then

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:40.030
use PD for-- many of those
databases have a mode to use

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:42.010
the block storage
as a raw device--

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:47.420
then you can potential to also
benefit from this 16 kB PD.

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:48.480
So what's the problem?

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:51.020
What's the problem we try
to solve at MySQL layer

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:52.710
with this 16 kB PD?

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:53.750
Here's the problem.

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:56.810
Here is a typical OLTP run.

00:21:56.810 --> 00:22:00.715
For those of you who use
MySQL and manages MySQL,

00:22:00.715 --> 00:22:05.370
the sysbench OLTP is a
standard benchmarking method

00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:08.280
to benchmark the performance
of MySQL database.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:12.170
This an eight hour run with
512 concurrent connections--

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.700
fairly heavy.

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:15.970
And as you can see, at the
second phase of the run,

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:19.100
the y-axis is the
performance DPS.

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:20.540
During the second
half of the run,

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:22.070
the performance varies greatly.

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:25.400
The database is, you
know, essentially not

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:29.120
that useful to you because the
performance varied by like 5x,

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:30.980
6x, and all that, right?

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:34.580
So now let's just do a
quick preview on what

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:38.180
you can achieve with a
16 kB PD and configure

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:41.790
the MySQL to disable that
double write feature.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:44.900
And so the middle of the
graph is the performance

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:47.660
after using 16 kB PD.

00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:49.190
As you can see,
it's more stable.

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:50.700
It's a little bit higher.

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:53.300
It's a bit higher than what's
the lower right corner, which

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.970
was before on the 4 kB pD.

00:22:55.970 --> 00:22:59.600
And then this is also our
internal regression test too.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:02.780
This was for the last
February and early March.

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:06.080
The blue lines here
are 16 kB performance,

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:07.940
MySQL running on 16 kB PD.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:11.870
And the red lines here are
MySQL running on 4 kB PD.

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:15.380
And 16 kB performance is a
bit higher and more stable

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:18.290
than the red lines,
than the 4 kB ones.

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:20.990
So OK, now let's just
dig a little bit deeper.

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:25.130
For those of you who never
touched MySQL before,

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:26.690
this might be boring.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:30.140
But you know, let's just
see where the problem is

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:33.870
and why the 16 kB PD will
help solve that problem.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:37.520
The problem here is if
internally inside MySQL,

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:40.220
this graph plotted is
what's called a flash lag.

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:43.580
Flash lag means that, you
know, how much lagging behind

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:46.800
is the 30 page flash
versus the log file.

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:48.620
So here, you can
see the MySQL log

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:50.120
file has grown to
the ceiling, which

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:51.890
is 20 megabytes in this case.

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.680
And MySQL is in a
sync flash state.

00:23:54.680 --> 00:23:59.390
In that state, MySQL will not
take new incoming mutations

00:23:59.390 --> 00:24:02.150
unless it can find a 30
page to flash, make room

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:04.640
in the log file, and
then take the mutation.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:07.280
This leads to the
initial problematic graph

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:09.380
where you see huge
variation of performance

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:14.340
because the database itself
is in a stop and go mode.

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.010
So in order to
control flash lag,

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:17.720
we want to make sure
that the 30 page

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:20.900
flash can be done at
a rate that's higher

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.530
than incoming write rate.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.280
Then inside MySQL, there's an
option called a double write.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:27.290
If we can disable
double write, then that

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:30.420
means we can reduce the 30
page flash rate by half,

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:32.210
essentially doubling
the IOPS you

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:34.700
can get on the 30
page flash front.

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:38.270
So then the next question will
become, what is double write,

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:43.080
and how does a 16 kB PD help
you disabled double write?

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:45.220
So double write--

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:47.430
InnoDB is a MySQL
storage engine.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:50.400
InnoDB double write
is to basically-- when

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:52.770
it needs to flash 30
pages, it actually

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:56.220
writes that page twice in two
different locations on disk

00:24:56.220 --> 00:25:00.210
because it needs to do that is
to guard against a torn page

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:01.060
situation.

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:03.270
A torn page situation
happens if you

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:07.950
write a 16 kB page on a
traditional 4 kB physical block

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:08.730
device.

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:12.390
During a failure mode,
maybe only part of the page

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:15.000
may be successfully written
on the disk like, say,

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:17.670
4 kB or 8 kB or 12 kB, right?

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.490
When that case happens,
the page is torn, is lost,

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:22.260
is only partially written.

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:25.140
You can't even replay
the transaction log

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.510
onto that page to
reconstruct a new page

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.610
because the old
page is essentially

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:31.230
overwritten and corrupted.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:34.920
So that's why InnoDB
needs to write the--

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.890
when they flash the 30 pages,
it needs to write twice--

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.670
first time to a backup
buffer called double

00:25:40.670 --> 00:25:43.080
write buffer, the second
page to the actual place.

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:45.810
If the second write
is torn and corrupted,

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.360
they can use the first
write to restore it.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:53.910
Now if we can build a
16 kB atomic write path

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:55.830
from database all
the way to the disk,

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:58.950
then we can avoid the need
to do this kind of double

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:03.200
write because all the 16 kB
writes would have been atomic.

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:05.670
It will happen end to end
all the way to the disk,

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.830
have all or nothing semantics.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:11.700
So that's where you
can use 16 kB PD for.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:13.090
Here are the list of steps.

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:14.050
Here are the details.

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:15.630
I'm not going to
repeat those steps.

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:17.670
You can just Google
best practices

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:20.580
for 16 kB persistent
disk and MySQL,

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:23.580
and those steps will
show up in a document.

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:26.160
But one quick thing I
want to call out here

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:29.970
is that the ability to create
a 16 kB persistent disk

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:32.190
is very unique on Google Cloud.

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:34.440
It moves back to the
initial architecture graph

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:39.720
in that persistent disk is a
fully virtualized storage block

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:43.620
device solution built on top
of a massive distributed file

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:45.150
system on the back end.

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:48.120
Because it's fully
utilized, it's

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:51.930
possible that we make
these kind of features

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:56.310
to customize a property called
physical block size that

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:59.400
in the physical world would
have been of attribute

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:01.320
of the underlying
physical device that

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.520
is extremely hard to customize.

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:08.280
So here is the 16 kB PD,
and here are the steps

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:11.580
that you can follow to
safely disable MySQL double

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:15.060
write by using a 16 kB PD.

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:16.550
One last thing I
want to call out--

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:18.120
that throughout
the steps, there's

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.550
a step mentioning that
you need to use container

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:25.250
optimized OS which
is called CoS.

00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:28.980
It is Google curated, and it's
supported by our Google team

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:29.900
here.

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:33.030
And it's designed for
Google Compute platform.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:36.030
One thing I want to mention
here is that it is also

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:39.570
used by Google Cloud SQL VMs.

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:42.210
The reason we want to
use CoS image if you

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:46.170
want to use 16 kB for MySQL is
that CoS image qualification

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:50.790
process has built-in tests to
ensure that the Linux kernel

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:53.580
layers between the file
system and the disk

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:58.860
do not split, break, misalign
the six writes across 16 KB

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:00.150
boundaries.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:03.030
So that is how we
ensure there is an end

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:06.900
to end atomic 16 kB write
path all the way to the disk.

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:09.950
